[18.065797, "o", "$ cat digest/$(date +%F).md\r\n"]
[19.072329, "o", "# arXiv Digest — 2026-10-06\r\n\r\n10 new papers.\r\n\r\n## AgentPrivArena: Evaluating and Auditing Real-world AI Agent Privacy\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06454v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- AgentPrivArena is a framework for evaluating LLM agent privacy that utilizes authentic MCP tools and self-hosted services within a reproducible environment to simulate realistic workflows.\r\n- The framework introduces trajectory-level privacy metrics designed to quantify unnecessary information access during multi-step execution, addressing the limitations of existing benchmarks that rely on simulated trajectories and outcome-based metrics.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Memory Canonicalization: A Framework and Benchmark for Cross-Model Drift in Persistent LLM Memory\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05124v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-04\r\n\r\n- The paper identifies a gap in persistent LLM memory systems where identical stored objects are interpreted differently by different models due to ambiguity, conditional structure, and emotional loading.\r\n- It proposes a write-time memory canonicalization pipeline that rewrites raw memory into an explicit, structurally disambiguated form.\r\n- The framework separates emotional valence into a distinct field to prevent interpretation based on tone, ensuring consistent factual and emotional processing across models.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## SparseCraft: Agentic Hardware-Software Co-Optimization for Sparse Computing\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05037v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-04\r\n\r\n- SparseCraft employs a language model within a closed CHIA loop to iteratively edit Chisel RTL, memory configurations, and sparse-kernel schedules for a Gemmini accelerator based on measured outcomes rather than analytical predictions.\r\n- The system enforces a rigorous validation pipeline where each candidate design must pass legality checks, cycle-accurate simulation, bit-for-bit comparison against a golden reference, and synthesis before being admitted.\r\n- The harness converts each measurement into a specific work order containing a diagnosed bottleneck and matching strategy guidance to direct the next iteration of hardware-software co-optimization.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## COPEX: Benchmarking LLM Robustness to Adversarial Context Across Model Context Protocol Layers\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04378v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-03\r\n\r\n- COPEX isolates the LLM as an MCP client by fixing the surrounding agent stack to distinguish model susceptibility from guardrails and orchestration capabilities.\r\n- The benchmark evaluates nine models across 3,375 trials involving 125 scenarios and 25 attack types across four entry surfaces, yielding a mean attack success rate of 64.4%.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## CyTReX: Explainable AI-Based Cybersecurity Threat Reasoning Framework for DER Networks\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04286v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-03\r\n\r\n- The framework addresses the limitation of single-label anomaly detection in Distributed Energy Resource networks by exposing alternative threat interpretations that are necessary for effective operational response.\r\n- CyTReX utilizes an evidence-grounded approach to transform network traffic data into explainable cybersecurity threat reasoning for edge and cloud systems.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Base Models Can Reason By Taking a Cue From Training Data\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06851v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- Fixing specific starting token cues, such as \".\\n\\nOkay\" or \"Alright,\" significantly boosts base model performance on math and coding tasks, making it competitive with reinforcement learning-trained counterparts.\r\n- Causal data interventions demonstrate that the reasoning effects of these token cues originate from associations formed in the training data, allowing arbitrary words to be transformed into effective reasoning triggers.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Learning to Read the Contextual Tokens in Diffusion Transformers\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06844v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- The authors introduce a framework that uses a lightweight bottleneck network to map intermediate contextual tokens from Multimodal"]
[19.07246, "o", " Diffusion Transformers into the input space of a frozen Large Language Model for natural-language interrogation.\r\n- This reader demonstrates that contextual tokens encode a rich, global representation of the emerging scene, including generation-specific semantics and attributes that remain underspecified in the original prompt.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Recursive Video In-Context Learning for Agentic Robot\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06843v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- The proposed Recursive Video In-Context Learning (RV-ICL) is a training-free method that structures demonstration videos into a navigable hierarchy of sub-events, such as grasps and releases, to address the limitations of static text memory and fixed keyframes.\r\n- This hierarchical approach allows the agent to dynamically access finer-grained visual details around specific contact points during execution, shifting focus from the overall task structure to the precise frames required for successful manipulation.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## UniSlider: Perceptually Uniform Sliders for Continuous Image Editing\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06831v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- The paper identifies that existing generative image editing sliders map linearly to strength parameters, causing non-monotonic trajectories where images revert or change abruptly rather than evolving smoothly.\r\n- UniSlider introduces a lightweight LoRA model that decouples the user-facing slider from the underlying strength parameter, enforcing a constraint that perceptual distance from the input grows linearly with the slider value.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Language models can notice an impossible engineering problem yet still report it as solved\r\n\r\n- URL: http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.06668v1\r\n- Published: 2026-10-05\r\n\r\n- The study evaluated 14 language models on 30 pairs of mechanics problems, where each pair consisted of a valid problem and a physically impossible variant created by altering a specific value or assumption.\r\n- Across three recent models, 12 out of 90 responses failed to reject the flawed problems, with 11 of these instances involving the model explicitly stating the flaw yet still providing an answer to the corrected version.\r\n\r\n---\r\n"]
[19.072789, "o", "\r\n"]
